1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
They're the tools that make work fun.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:10,000
Manufactured by household names and Hollywood celebrities.

3
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:16,000
Easier to use and more powerful than ever.

4
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:20,000
But they weren't always like this.

5
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:24,000
Now, Power Tools on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:48,826 --> 00:00:50,300
Hi, I'm Ron Hazleton.

7
00:00:50,640 --> 00:00:52,060
Welcome to the Tool Bench.

8
00:00:52,520 --> 00:00:55,860
You know, my dad built his own house when he was only 19 years old.

9
00:00:55,861 --> 00:01:00,540
and that was long before you could run out to the local mega home center to pick up a good power tool.

10
00:01:01,360 --> 00:01:04,060
I gained a real respect for hand tools from my dad,

11
00:01:04,380 --> 00:01:06,980
but I don't think I'd ever want to give up my power tools.

12
00:01:07,420 --> 00:01:11,280
I've gotten so used to them, it's hard to imagine a time when they weren't around.

13
00:01:11,800 --> 00:01:16,440
But in the long history of tools, power, well, that's just the tip of the iceberg.

14
00:01:17,960 --> 00:01:20,580
Beginning with primitive tools made from rocks,

15
00:01:20,581 --> 00:01:26,540
human civilization could be measured by how we make, use, and improve tools.

16
00:01:27,360 --> 00:01:30,600
From stone to copper and later iron and steel,

17
00:01:30,980 --> 00:01:34,720
we've been making tools for at least two and a half million years.

18
00:01:36,460 --> 00:01:43,460
At the very end of this spectrum, about .0004% of the tool timeline

19
00:01:43,461 --> 00:01:46,760
are power tools.

20
00:01:46,761 --> 00:01:57,760
The ability to motorize and electrify tools made it possible to build houses, skyscrapers,

21
00:01:57,761 --> 00:02:04,760
and entire communities with unprecedented speed and efficiency.

22
00:02:04,761 --> 00:02:08,760
They've also made using tools just a little more exciting.

23
00:02:08,761 --> 00:02:11,760
I think the mystique is all about power and speed.

24
00:02:11,761 --> 00:02:16,760
speed and I don't know that males are more interested in power and speed than females

25
00:02:16,761 --> 00:02:21,480
but I think everybody is interested in getting a job done more quickly and more efficiently.

26
00:02:22,680 --> 00:02:27,900
Today the power tool industry is booming thanks to the steady introduction of tools that are

27
00:02:27,901 --> 00:02:33,820
lighter, cordless and more powerful than ever, which for many is the best part of all.

28
00:02:36,320 --> 00:02:39,460
There's a guy I know that doesn't say more horsepower would be better,

29
00:02:39,461 --> 00:02:41,200
bigger, badder lawnmower.

30
00:02:41,360 --> 00:02:44,640
And most of the guys I know will modify just about anything.

31
00:02:45,340 --> 00:02:48,920
Lawnmower, you know, if you take the Venturi out of that carburetor,

32
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:50,160
you can get a little bit more horsepower.

33
00:02:50,280 --> 00:02:52,200
Is there any reason to get more horsepower out of a lawnmower?

34
00:02:52,260 --> 00:02:52,960
I don't think so.

35
00:02:53,660 --> 00:02:55,320
But always more horsepower is better.

36
00:02:56,880 --> 00:03:00,000
Powered by stronger batteries and more efficient motors,

37
00:03:00,480 --> 00:03:04,500
today's portable cordless power tools can perform just as effectively

38
00:03:04,501 --> 00:03:06,540
as their cord-bound predecessors.

39
00:03:06,541 --> 00:03:09,760
and batteries are not the only energy source.

40
00:03:10,340 --> 00:03:12,380
Some tools are powered by compressed air.

41
00:03:14,480 --> 00:03:19,700
The Stanley Bostitch Company is most commonly associated with desktop staplers,

42
00:03:19,701 --> 00:03:22,360
but at their East Greenwich, Rhode Island plant,

43
00:03:22,760 --> 00:03:27,880
they produce some of the best-selling pneumatic nail and staple guns on the market today.

44
00:03:27,881 --> 00:03:41,881
This roofing stapler, powered by a 90 pounds per square inch burst of compressed air, can drive a row of staples in seconds.

45
00:03:43,160 --> 00:03:47,580
And it's designed to never jam, no matter how inept the user.

46
00:03:48,500 --> 00:03:50,100
It can also take quite a beating.

47
00:03:50,101 --> 00:03:57,200
This tool was designed with marketing specs that required that it be dropped from the

48
00:03:57,201 --> 00:04:04,440
peak of a roof 50 times on its nose so that it can fall down like that and nothing happens.

49
00:04:04,441 --> 00:04:10,580
A different type of nail gun is manufactured at the Porter Cable Plant in Jackson, Tennessee.

50
00:04:10,581 --> 00:04:17,300
The BAMR is powered not by compressed air, but by compressed liquefied gas, also known

51
00:04:17,301 --> 00:04:19,600
as a fuel cell.

52
00:04:19,601 --> 00:04:23,420
The advantage of this energy source is that it doesn't have to be connected to an air

53
00:04:23,421 --> 00:04:28,100
compressor to work.

54
00:04:28,101 --> 00:04:36,380
Meanwhile at the Ryobi plant in Pickens, South Carolina, a reciprocating saw that could tear

55
00:04:36,381 --> 00:04:42,300
through walls runs off liquid gasoline just like a lawn mower.

56
00:04:42,301 --> 00:04:49,120
But for the vast majority of power tool users, the preferred energy source is still the battery.

57
00:04:49,121 --> 00:04:57,760
Thanks to the power of today's batteries, circular saws can cut through wood like a knife through butter.

58
00:04:58,700 --> 00:05:03,180
Drill bits can be powered to bore holes just as fast as you can pull the trigger.

59
00:05:05,660 --> 00:05:11,560
But behind the story of circular saws and drill bits are two now forgotten names.

60
00:05:12,120 --> 00:05:15,800
They made their marks on the tool industry before we even had electricity.

61
00:05:15,801 --> 00:05:24,180
One was a woman, the other a blacksmith with a fondness for whiskey, and their influence is still felt today.

62
00:05:26,520 --> 00:05:32,520
In the 1800s, the Shakers, a religious community that had flourished in the eastern United States,

63
00:05:33,120 --> 00:05:37,100
were well known for their exceptional furniture and woodworking skills.

64
00:05:37,640 --> 00:05:43,440
But it was a Shaker sister named Tabitha Babbitt who in 1810 got a great idea.

65
00:05:43,441 --> 00:05:46,880
It came to her while working at her spinning wheel.

66
00:05:47,800 --> 00:05:57,680
She worked around a lot of men who toiled day in and day out sawing logs the way that they did it for a century,

67
00:05:57,860 --> 00:06:01,020
which was basically they would take a log and position it over a pit.

68
00:06:01,980 --> 00:06:09,160
And one man was in the pit and one man was above, and they took a large handsaw and simply sawed through the log.

69
00:06:09,161 --> 00:06:11,980
and of course she saw that that was incredibly labor-intensive

70
00:06:11,981 --> 00:06:15,040
and very hard work.

71
00:06:15,600 --> 00:06:17,920
And so she came up with the idea of,

72
00:06:18,140 --> 00:06:20,260
let's take that straight blade, put it on a wheel.

73
00:06:21,680 --> 00:06:25,380
It occurred to Babbitt that a circular revolving blade

74
00:06:25,381 --> 00:06:28,340
could cut wood more efficiently than hand saws.

75
00:06:29,080 --> 00:06:31,760
Her idea was adopted by Shaker woodworkers

76
00:06:31,761 --> 00:06:34,500
who powered the circular saws by hand cranks

77
00:06:34,501 --> 00:06:36,720
and later by water wheels.

78
00:06:37,600 --> 00:06:41,520
So think of Tabitha the next time you pick up that circular saw blade,

79
00:06:42,080 --> 00:06:45,240
the one you probably assumed was invented by a man.

80
00:06:46,820 --> 00:06:51,320
The next time you change a drill bit, you might think of this Ohio blacksmith.

81
00:06:52,280 --> 00:06:58,640
In 1884, William Mac Dimmett took out a patent on a drill bit that he forged out of steel.

82
00:06:59,600 --> 00:07:03,880
Prior to Dimmett, drill bits were either similar to straight, tapered files,

83
00:07:04,200 --> 00:07:06,160
or they had a screw-like twist.

84
00:07:06,720 --> 00:07:13,420
What made Demet's unique was that it had a solid stem running through the middle of a screw-like twist.

85
00:07:14,220 --> 00:07:18,620
Demet probably didn't realize that he'd created one of the best bits ever made.

86
00:07:19,420 --> 00:07:23,480
But today, you'll never find William Demet's name in a hardware store.

87
00:07:23,980 --> 00:07:26,200
But you will find his drill bit.

88
00:07:28,700 --> 00:07:30,640
Here's my favorite drill bit.

89
00:07:31,160 --> 00:07:35,740
This is an Erwin Auger, which sounds like a cartoon character name, but it's not.

90
00:07:35,741 --> 00:07:38,640
It's a guy named Charles Irwin who was a pharmacist in Ohio.

91
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:41,740
Now, how does a pharmacist get a drill bit?

92
00:07:42,160 --> 00:07:46,340
Well, this was back in the 1880s when drugstores actually sold alcoholic beverages.

93
00:07:47,060 --> 00:07:54,080
One of his customers was William Demet, who loved to come in every day and consume some of these alcoholic beverages,

94
00:07:54,560 --> 00:07:56,560
ran up a bill and then couldn't pay it.

95
00:07:57,140 --> 00:07:58,540
He had invented this.

96
00:07:58,740 --> 00:08:00,860
William Demet had invented this drill bit.

97
00:08:00,861 --> 00:08:07,140
So he traded the patent on this to Charles Irwin for his back bill of alcohol.

98
00:08:08,380 --> 00:08:15,480
William Demet sold his patent to Charles Irwin for the dismissal of his $20 back bill in a bottle of whiskey.

99
00:08:16,420 --> 00:08:19,940
Irwin immediately formed a company to manufacture the drill bit.

100
00:08:19,941 --> 00:08:32,300
Still manufactured today, about 100 million Erwin auger drill bits have been sold since the day William Dimmett traded his patent for a whiskey debt.

101
00:08:34,220 --> 00:08:45,100
The contributions of Tabitha Babbitt and William Dimmett aside, it wasn't until 1895 that the world would see the first portable electric power tool.

102
00:08:45,101 --> 00:08:53,600
It came a full 16 years after Thomas Edison harnessed electricity to power the incandescent

103
00:08:53,601 --> 00:08:56,260
electric lamp.

104
00:08:56,261 --> 00:09:01,920
The tool was a drill invented by the German engineering firm of C&E Fine.

105
00:09:01,921 --> 00:09:08,320
It weighed 16 and a half pounds and was powered not only by its DC motor, but by the weight

106
00:09:08,321 --> 00:09:09,820
of the user.

107
00:09:09,821 --> 00:09:18,380
There's a chest plate back here, and the way that would work is that you would hold it with both your hands and press that up against your chest.

108
00:09:18,381 --> 00:09:27,740
And because it didn't spin all that fast, you had to use the pressure from your chest to force that drill into the workpiece.

109
00:09:28,280 --> 00:09:37,820
It would take about another 20 years before two men would significantly improve the electric drill and become household names as a result.

110
00:09:38,720 --> 00:09:40,480
The year was 1910.

111
00:09:40,940 --> 00:09:45,320
A tool and die worker named Duncan Black sold his car for $600.

112
00:09:46,240 --> 00:09:51,900
He used the money to open a machine shop in Baltimore with his friend Alonzo Decker.

113
00:09:52,880 --> 00:09:57,520
Thoughts of designing a new type of drill had intrigued them, but they weren't sure how to do it.

114
00:09:58,100 --> 00:10:03,180
Then, in 1914, an idea seemed to consume them both at the same moment.

115
00:10:03,181 --> 00:10:09,020
They were both in my father's kitchen one Saturday, and they were sitting around a table

116
00:10:09,021 --> 00:10:14,740
trying to get a bright idea of how they could do this.

117
00:10:14,741 --> 00:10:19,340
And father had a Colt automatic laying on the kitchen table.

118
00:10:19,341 --> 00:10:24,980
And as they were looking at the sky or the stars or whatever, each one of them happened

119
00:10:24,981 --> 00:10:27,840
to look at that Colt automatic.

120
00:10:27,841 --> 00:10:34,740
And so my mother tells me that the two of them said, that's it, at the same time.

121
00:10:35,280 --> 00:10:41,980
The epiphany Black & Decker had was to adapt the design of the Colt 45 to the electric drill.

122
00:10:42,860 --> 00:10:47,700
In 1916, they began marketing the first pistol grip trigger switch drills.

123
00:10:48,380 --> 00:10:54,600
This is one of the first Black & Decker drills housed at the company headquarters in Townsend, Maryland.

124
00:10:54,601 --> 00:11:00,600
Black & Decker's design resulted in a lighter drill that could be handled by one man.

125
00:11:00,601 --> 00:11:05,600
The motor was smaller and more powerful than those used on the German drills.

126
00:11:05,601 --> 00:11:09,600
Suddenly the power tool industry was off and running,

127
00:11:09,601 --> 00:11:15,600
and right on the heels of Black & Decker would come other familiar names.

128
00:11:15,601 --> 00:11:21,600
In 1969, the Erwin Augerbit plant came to a screeching halt

129
00:11:21,601 --> 00:11:25,992
halt when a runaway train crashed through the factory wall.

130
00:11:25,993 --> 00:11:28,300
Remarkably, no one was injured.

131
00:11:32,500 --> 00:11:38,780
Today, almost everyone has used a power drill, but one of the oldest power tools might be the

132
00:11:38,781 --> 00:11:44,164
least familiar to the novice do-it-yourselfer. This is a router.

133
00:11:44,165 --> 00:11:46,520
These small, round tools are

134
00:11:46,521 --> 00:11:52,762
used to spin a variety of bits to cut grooves into wood pieces like doors or cabinets.

135
00:11:52,763 --> 00:11:53,463
The

136
00:11:53,464 --> 00:11:58,041
router was invented three years after Black & Decker's pistol grip drill.

137
00:11:58,042 --> 00:11:59,220
The year was 1919

138
00:11:59,221 --> 00:12:03,491
and the inventor was a pattern maker named R.L. Carter.

139
00:12:03,492 --> 00:12:06,520
This now made it possible for people to

140
00:12:06,521 --> 00:12:12,480
put molded profiles on the edges of work pieces, whereas before they had to have very expensive,

141
00:12:12,481 --> 00:12:18,980
very elaborate hand planes which were difficult to sharpen, difficult to operate, and now

142
00:12:18,981 --> 00:12:26,800
you had one electric tool that could do it and almost anybody could operate it.

143
00:12:26,801 --> 00:12:30,740
Carter came up with the idea for the router while reworking an electric barber's clipper

144
00:12:30,741 --> 00:12:32,800
in his garage.

145
00:12:32,801 --> 00:12:39,600
In the years to follow, there would be a steady stream of power tool innovations.

146
00:12:39,601 --> 00:12:45,060
Herbert Totts entered the field in 1923 from his Milwaukee, Wisconsin garage.

147
00:12:45,820 --> 00:12:50,220
There he invented the earliest version of this tool, the scroll saw.

148
00:12:50,580 --> 00:12:55,440
The scroll saw has a cutting blade that reciprocates in an up-and-down fashion.

149
00:12:56,200 --> 00:12:59,400
Totts called his model the American Boy Scroll Saw.

150
00:12:59,520 --> 00:13:03,140
The user cranked a handle to put the saw in motion.

151
00:13:03,141 --> 00:13:12,980
But Tots also sold a scroll saw with a 1 20th horsepower motor in place of the crank.

152
00:13:12,981 --> 00:13:19,540
The manual saw sold for $6 and was popular with hobbyists, while the motorized version

153
00:13:19,541 --> 00:13:24,500
was sold to professionals for four times as much.

154
00:13:24,501 --> 00:13:27,860
Tots' company no longer operates out of a garage.

155
00:13:27,861 --> 00:13:32,140
Today, it's the Delta Corporation, a leader in the industry.

156
00:13:33,200 --> 00:13:38,820
In 1918, automobile maker Henry Ford challenged a young tool manufacturer, A.H.

157
00:13:38,821 --> 00:13:39,521
Peterson,

158
00:13:39,522 --> 00:13:44,880
to produce a smaller, lighter electric drill for long days on the assembly line.

159
00:13:48,420 --> 00:13:52,040
Peterson delighted Ford with a five-pound electric drill,

160
00:13:52,660 --> 00:13:55,620
capable of handling the heavy demands of the car industry.

161
00:13:55,621 --> 00:14:00,300
It was also the first drill that could be operated with only one hand.

162
00:14:01,120 --> 00:14:03,480
Peterson called it the hole shooter.

163
00:14:05,380 --> 00:14:10,560
In 1924, facing financial difficulties caused by a fire at his plant,

164
00:14:11,120 --> 00:14:13,760
Peterson sold the hole shooter to A.F. Siebert,

165
00:14:14,160 --> 00:14:17,360
who used the drill to begin the Milwaukee Tool Company.

166
00:14:18,200 --> 00:14:22,840
A pioneer in the power saw industry got its start in the 20s as well.

167
00:14:23,580 --> 00:14:26,700
That's when an inventor from Louisiana, Edmund Michel,

168
00:14:27,160 --> 00:14:31,320
stood in the sugar cane field and was appalled by what he witnessed.

169
00:14:32,860 --> 00:14:36,160
Cutting sugar cane was a brutally difficult job.

170
00:14:37,020 --> 00:14:43,400
Field hands withering in the hot sun would swing machetes over and over again to cut the thick cane.

171
00:14:45,060 --> 00:14:49,460
Michel set out to ease the grueling work by attempting to motorize a machete.

172
00:14:49,461 --> 00:14:56,460
But, like Tabitha Babbitt, he eventually concluded that a round blade would work even better.

173
00:14:56,461 --> 00:15:05,460
Using the motor from a malted milk mixer to power the blade, his hand-held circular saw barely cut through a cigar box.

174
00:15:05,461 --> 00:15:08,460
But Michel would improve upon his invention.

175
00:15:08,461 --> 00:15:16,460
In 1924, he began selling his 10-pound skill saw for an astronomical $160.

176
00:15:16,461 --> 00:15:19,840
In today's economy, that would be more than $1,500.

177
00:15:20,780 --> 00:15:24,240
And even at that price, it had serious limitations.

178
00:15:25,080 --> 00:15:28,760
The first models would only last seven or eight hours on the job site.

179
00:15:28,960 --> 00:15:32,160
But even with that limitation, the contractors welcomed that,

180
00:15:32,500 --> 00:15:35,020
even though every day they'd have to take it back to skill

181
00:15:35,021 --> 00:15:38,580
and have it completely fixed,

182
00:15:38,880 --> 00:15:42,260
have bearings and switches and internal parts replaced.

183
00:15:42,360 --> 00:15:44,780
It was still worth it to them because of the time savings.

184
00:15:45,560 --> 00:15:51,720
In 1926, a young inventor was inspired by Edmund Michel's success with the skill saw.

185
00:15:52,480 --> 00:15:57,480
At the time, Art Emmons was working for the Porter Cable Company in Syracuse, New York.

186
00:15:58,200 --> 00:16:04,240
Named after the company's two founders, Porter Cable started in 1906 making pencil sharpeners.

187
00:16:04,920 --> 00:16:09,940
And of course, like other early tool manufacturers, they made them out of a garage.

188
00:16:09,941 --> 00:16:14,700
But by the 20s, Art Emmons believed the promise of power tools was great.

189
00:16:14,780 --> 00:16:20,020
Emmons envisioned a way to solve the labor-intensive jobs of sanding and finishing.

190
00:16:21,100 --> 00:16:25,540
Up until that time, all sanding had to take place on large stationary machines.

191
00:16:25,840 --> 00:16:30,680
So he felt that if you could design a product that instead of either sanding by hand

192
00:16:30,681 --> 00:16:34,400
or having to pick something large up and carry it to a machine to be sanded,

193
00:16:34,620 --> 00:16:36,400
it would greatly improve productivity.

194
00:16:39,520 --> 00:16:43,960
In 1926, Emmons invented the first portable belt sanding.

195
00:16:44,780 --> 00:16:50,420
Weighing less than 15 pounds, it enabled workers to carry the tool to the work instead of vice versa.

196
00:16:51,500 --> 00:16:53,960
Because of this, Emmons called it the takeabout.

197
00:16:54,240 --> 00:16:59,760
It would garner the young inventor the first of more than 50 patents for tools and parts.

198
00:17:01,220 --> 00:17:05,860
Emmons' takeabout would be Porter Cable's best-selling tool for decades to come.

199
00:17:06,820 --> 00:17:10,520
He designed it with features that would take competitors years to equal.

200
00:17:11,480 --> 00:17:15,680
He installed a dust collection system and positioned a motor in such a way

201
00:17:15,681 --> 00:17:19,320
that the takeabout could even sand flush up against baseboards.

202
00:17:20,380 --> 00:17:25,240
Thanks to the innovations of the 1920s, the power tool industry was growing,

203
00:17:25,840 --> 00:17:30,100
even in a world where whole communities were still without electricity.

204
00:17:30,500 --> 00:17:35,780
But just as this new industry was gaining momentum, turmoil was about to strike.

205
00:17:36,520 --> 00:17:37,840
And it would strike hard.

206
00:17:40,520 --> 00:17:48,180
It's estimated that there are 106,000 power tool accidents requiring medical attention in the United States each year.

207
00:17:57,080 --> 00:17:59,380
Now this is my idea of a power tool.

208
00:17:59,820 --> 00:18:03,780
Today you can buy a tool like this from stores, catalogs, or Internet services.

209
00:18:04,140 --> 00:18:07,500
And you can just as easily get any advice you need on how to use it.

210
00:18:08,140 --> 00:18:10,840
This being the information age, information is everywhere.

211
00:18:11,580 --> 00:18:14,980
But imagine being one of the first power tool salesmen

212
00:18:14,981 --> 00:18:18,120
at a time when a lot of people didn't even have electricity

213
00:18:18,121 --> 00:18:21,220
and your tool cost a month's salary.

214
00:18:22,120 --> 00:18:23,040
Pretty tough sell.

215
00:18:25,420 --> 00:18:28,620
When a power tool company offers a new product today,

216
00:18:29,140 --> 00:18:31,800
they can show it off to millions of people at once.

217
00:18:32,420 --> 00:18:34,800
All it takes is one TV commercial.

218
00:18:35,680 --> 00:18:37,760
But it wasn't always this easy.

219
00:18:38,740 --> 00:18:43,340
During the 1920s and 30s, when the power tool industry was in its infancy,

220
00:18:44,060 --> 00:18:49,760
there was no television nor gigantic home centers with employees trained to use the tool.

221
00:18:50,560 --> 00:18:53,900
So how did these fledgling power tool companies sell their product?

222
00:18:55,620 --> 00:18:58,980
They had salespeople who used to put these tools in the trunk of their car,

223
00:18:58,981 --> 00:19:02,300
and they'd drive around the streets of their community looking for job sites

224
00:19:02,301 --> 00:19:04,620
or where contractors or professionals were being used.

225
00:19:04,800 --> 00:19:08,780
And they would go in and they would go ahead and show the features and the benefits of this product

226
00:19:08,781 --> 00:19:12,460
and convince the owner that he had to part with often months' wages,

227
00:19:12,560 --> 00:19:15,720
maybe even two months' wages to buy one of these newfangled electric tools,

228
00:19:16,140 --> 00:19:17,620
but he could always sell the productivity.

229
00:19:18,440 --> 00:19:23,140
As if it wasn't enough to convince someone to give up a month's salary for a tool,

230
00:19:23,680 --> 00:19:27,500
these determined salesmen had an even bigger obstacle in their way.

231
00:19:28,380 --> 00:19:30,860
Many of the markets they went to didn't even have electricity,

232
00:19:30,861 --> 00:19:35,200
so a lot of the salesmen, it said, would carry portable generators that were gas-driven

233
00:19:35,201 --> 00:19:39,460
so they could fire up the generator to show the particular individual or prospective buyer

234
00:19:39,461 --> 00:19:41,600
what this tool would do once they got electricity.

235
00:19:41,960 --> 00:19:43,660
So you can imagine what a hard sell that was.

236
00:19:44,300 --> 00:19:48,440
When it needed it the most, the industry would receive an important boost

237
00:19:48,441 --> 00:19:51,840
in the form of instant mass marketing and credibility.

238
00:19:52,820 --> 00:19:58,140
In 1928, Sears Roebuck began offering power tools in their popular mail order catalog.

239
00:19:58,141 --> 00:20:04,280
It was a time when many Americans still lived in far-flung farming communities

240
00:20:04,281 --> 00:20:08,620
and did not have many choices or opportunities for buying new products.

241
00:20:09,280 --> 00:20:14,420
But when the catalog arrived, it was an exciting chance to see how the world was changing

242
00:20:14,421 --> 00:20:17,080
from the swing on their own front porch.

243
00:20:17,760 --> 00:20:21,080
They would arrive and they would spend days, weeks, social hours,

244
00:20:21,200 --> 00:20:24,980
turning the pages of that catalog looking at these new innovative products.

245
00:20:25,440 --> 00:20:29,400
And here you are in an agricultural community or in a farming community,

246
00:20:29,401 --> 00:20:33,320
and you see a drill or anything that could make your job easier.

247
00:20:33,600 --> 00:20:36,960
Once electricity got there, it was like the things dreams are made of.

248
00:20:37,000 --> 00:20:38,660
You'd sit there and you'd think about it at night.

249
00:20:38,820 --> 00:20:40,900
Because remember, there was no Monday night football.

250
00:20:41,460 --> 00:20:45,240
But in the blink of an eye, the power tool industry's fortunes,

251
00:20:45,340 --> 00:20:48,860
along with everyone else's, would take a change for the worse.

252
00:20:49,660 --> 00:20:54,260
On October 29, 1929, the bottom fell out of the stock market,

253
00:20:54,261 --> 00:21:02,620
and neither Alonzo Decker's pistol grip drill nor Art Emmons' takeabout sander could do anything to fix this mess.

254
00:21:03,560 --> 00:21:12,480
With the Depression, jobs dried up and professionals could no longer afford expensive tools because they had no place to use them.

255
00:21:14,020 --> 00:21:18,900
The story at Black & Decker was the same as it was at other power tool companies.

256
00:21:19,840 --> 00:21:21,060
It was about layoffs.

257
00:21:21,800 --> 00:21:24,560
And the first to go was a recent college graduate,

258
00:21:25,060 --> 00:21:28,400
the son of the founding partner, Al Decker Jr.

259
00:21:29,500 --> 00:21:32,700
My father called me in and said,

260
00:21:32,800 --> 00:21:35,940
Son, we're going to have to lay off an awful lot of people

261
00:21:35,941 --> 00:21:38,920
because business is really going to hell in a handbasket.

262
00:21:39,240 --> 00:21:44,080
And I said, and I'm not going to lay off anybody else as long as you're here,

263
00:21:44,240 --> 00:21:45,700
so you're going to have to be number one.

264
00:21:46,120 --> 00:21:47,680
I said, I understand, Father.

265
00:21:47,681 --> 00:21:49,120
Okay.

266
00:21:50,400 --> 00:21:54,481
In 1932, the newly elected president, Franklin D.

267
00:21:54,482 --> 00:21:57,480
Roosevelt, was promising a new deal.

268
00:21:57,660 --> 00:21:59,020
Keep that faith high.

269
00:21:59,900 --> 00:22:04,100
So shall we win through to a better day.

270
00:22:04,940 --> 00:22:11,100
And even though the country was still mired in financial turmoil, a sense of optimism arose.

271
00:22:11,101 --> 00:22:16,780
And most importantly to builders, the power tool industry offered a product that was essential

272
00:22:16,781 --> 00:22:19,360
to the promise of future growth.

273
00:22:21,540 --> 00:22:23,560
Months after FDR's election,

274
00:22:23,880 --> 00:22:27,840
Sears offered their first catalog dedicated to power tools.

275
00:22:28,820 --> 00:22:32,560
Craftsman would become a brand name for a variety of power tools

276
00:22:32,561 --> 00:22:36,400
made by different tool makers to meet the marketer's requirements.

277
00:22:37,400 --> 00:22:41,700
Even though power tools were still predominantly bought by professionals,

278
00:22:42,180 --> 00:22:45,060
they were sneaking their way into the consumer market.

279
00:22:45,061 --> 00:22:46,200
In 1932, A.J.

280
00:22:46,201 --> 00:23:00,060
Dremel, a Racine, Wisconsin engineer and inventor, launched a company from, you guessed it, his garage, to produce and sell his small moto tool for hobbyists.

281
00:23:01,020 --> 00:23:07,480
Marketed as a hundred tools in one, it would grind, route, and polish.

282
00:23:08,040 --> 00:23:15,700
It's still made in Racine by the Dremel Company and today performs all those jobs and many others.

283
00:23:19,040 --> 00:23:28,080
Companies like Milwaukee, Black & Decker, Porter Cable, and Sears had no sooner emerged from the Depression when they were plunged into World War II.

284
00:23:28,081 --> 00:23:31,080
The experience at Porter Cable was typical.

285
00:23:31,081 --> 00:23:37,512
Still yearning to reach a mass market, their primary mission, now, was meeting the demands of the U.S.

286
00:23:37,513 --> 00:23:38,213
military.

287
00:23:38,214 --> 00:23:44,080
Porter Cable got into the effort by supplying what was known as the G-8 Belt Sander.

288
00:23:44,081 --> 00:23:51,080
And this was a large floor standing machine that was used in the manufacturing tank and heavy armament as well as the aircraft industry.

289
00:23:51,081 --> 00:23:55,260
We were also called into service to make things that we had no knowledge or practicality of,

290
00:23:55,500 --> 00:24:00,700
such as valves for torpedoes, aircraft parts, as well as parachute ripcords.

291
00:24:03,740 --> 00:24:07,660
Meanwhile at Black & Decker, Al Jr. was brought back to the company

292
00:24:07,661 --> 00:24:11,140
and was rising quickly through the executive ranks.

293
00:24:11,740 --> 00:24:13,500
He was offered a commission in the Army,

294
00:24:13,980 --> 00:24:17,800
but the government declared his work at Black & Decker essential to the war effort,

295
00:24:17,801 --> 00:24:26,040
So Decker stayed and fought the war from the factory floor, where the demand for electric drills was higher than ever before.

296
00:24:26,780 --> 00:24:32,020
In building airplanes, all the holes were all drilled with electric drills.

297
00:24:32,460 --> 00:24:37,420
So we began getting orders for electric drills that were just out of this world.

298
00:24:38,720 --> 00:24:45,640
During the war, Decker received some information that he would tuck away for an important innovation once the war was over.

299
00:24:45,641 --> 00:24:50,960
When the whistle blew, airplane factory employees frequently took their black and

300
00:24:50,961 --> 00:24:54,174
deckered grills home with them. They weren't stealing.

301
00:24:54,175 --> 00:24:55,960
It's just that during the war,

302
00:24:56,100 --> 00:25:02,780
the government implored people with a new national logo, fix it yourself. Plumbers,

303
00:25:03,040 --> 00:25:07,975
electricians, and repairmen were away in the service or in defense jobs.

304
00:25:07,976 --> 00:25:09,140
Department stores

305
00:25:09,141 --> 00:25:14,980
and community centers organized special workshops to teach people how to do it themselves.

306
00:25:15,640 --> 00:25:24,820
After World War II, all this influx of soldiers returning from the war, buying these little

307
00:25:24,821 --> 00:25:29,620
houses, they're still working, they don't really have all that much money, and that's

308
00:25:29,621 --> 00:25:32,520
how the do-it-yourself movement sort of got started.

309
00:25:32,521 --> 00:25:36,200
These guys were buying these houses, building these houses, and they were fixing them up

310
00:25:36,201 --> 00:25:37,200
themselves.

311
00:25:37,201 --> 00:25:43,100
After the war, Decker already knew, based on his experience with the airline workers

312
00:25:43,101 --> 00:25:49,820
taking drills home that there was a market for consumer-oriented power tools.

313
00:25:49,821 --> 00:25:54,580
In 1946, Black and Decker introduced just such a line.

314
00:25:54,581 --> 00:26:00,340
They were crafted from lighter weight metals, were smaller, easier to grasp, and had lower

315
00:26:00,341 --> 00:26:03,300
amperage than the tools that had built the war machine.

316
00:26:03,301 --> 00:26:08,340
They didn't have to be of the same quality because instead of drilling hundreds of holes

317
00:26:08,341 --> 00:26:14,140
daily for airplanes, these were used for weekend household chores.

318
00:26:14,141 --> 00:26:20,240
The compromise in size and power also made the tools affordable for consumers.

319
00:26:20,241 --> 00:26:25,220
Within three years, the company sold over one million quarter-inch drills.

320
00:26:25,221 --> 00:26:32,600
It was much more successful than we ever dreamed that it was going to be.

321
00:26:32,601 --> 00:26:39,620
And we followed up with a very expanded line of products for all of those people who do it themselves.

322
00:26:40,380 --> 00:26:43,880
And it's been an excellent market, and still is.

323
00:26:45,300 --> 00:26:50,000
1946 would also see the introduction of another popular power tool.

324
00:26:50,860 --> 00:26:56,080
A Swiss employee of what is now the Skill Bosch Corporation invented this tool,

325
00:26:56,900 --> 00:27:00,760
a handheld version of the scroll saw, the jigsaw.

326
00:27:00,761 --> 00:27:03,840
But it didn't look like this at the time.

327
00:27:05,660 --> 00:27:08,900
Albert Kaufman was attempting to repair his wife's sewing machine.

328
00:27:09,720 --> 00:27:12,760
While working on the machine, my guess, as he was in the garage,

329
00:27:13,400 --> 00:27:14,740
Kaufman had an idea.

330
00:27:15,620 --> 00:27:19,860
He would replace the up-and-down reciprocating stitch rig with a blade.

331
00:27:20,780 --> 00:27:25,200
The result was a portable saw capable of making intricate cuts in wood.

332
00:27:25,201 --> 00:27:34,320
Yet, of all the developments that came out of the post-war years, the most significant

333
00:27:34,321 --> 00:27:38,620
was a new material that would revolutionize power tools.

334
00:27:38,621 --> 00:27:43,980
It would not only make them lighter and less expensive, it would help prevent millions

335
00:27:43,981 --> 00:27:49,740
from having a shocking experience.

336
00:27:49,741 --> 00:27:55,020
In addition to introducing several first-of-their-kind power tools, Porter Cable was unique in the

337
00:27:55,021 --> 00:28:01,760
1930s for offering employee benefits, including stock ownership and an on-site bowling alley.

338
00:28:04,520 --> 00:28:06,833
Electricity and I have a thing.

339
00:28:06,834 --> 00:28:11,160
I've gotten shocked more than I care to admit because I've

340
00:28:11,161 --> 00:28:12,488
tried to do things the wrong way.

341
00:28:12,489 --> 00:28:14,660
If you're going to work on electrical things, stay out

342
00:28:14,661 --> 00:28:17,512
of water and shut the circuit breaker off.

343
00:28:17,513 --> 00:28:20,500
Even if you heeded Tim Allen's advice, prior

344
00:28:20,501 --> 00:28:26,820
Prior to the 1960s, a power tool might have given you a nasty shock anyway.

345
00:28:26,821 --> 00:28:32,020
Back then, the only protection between the user and an electrical shock was the grounding

346
00:28:32,021 --> 00:28:41,880
wire that ran through the third prong of a power cord.

347
00:28:41,881 --> 00:28:47,000
Plastic would become the key to making power tools not only safer, but lighter and more

348
00:28:47,001 --> 00:28:48,000
durable.

349
00:28:48,001 --> 00:28:54,900
However, when plastic first replaced metal on power tools in the 1960s, it left many suspicious.

350
00:28:55,840 --> 00:29:02,280
People associated plastic with the terrible stench that arose when a toy fell behind a radiator and melted.

351
00:29:03,740 --> 00:29:06,820
That was the image that plastic had in that time frame.

352
00:29:07,120 --> 00:29:08,760
We weren't using those kinds of plastics.

353
00:29:08,761 --> 00:29:15,140
And our pitch to our customers was that if you ride in an airplane,

354
00:29:15,520 --> 00:29:19,280
quite probable the windows are made out of the same material as our drills are made out of.

355
00:29:20,060 --> 00:29:26,480
The plastic used on both airplane windows and power tool housings is a polycarbonate resin,

356
00:29:27,080 --> 00:29:30,460
which is much stronger than the softer plastic used on toys.

357
00:29:31,600 --> 00:29:35,980
Plastic manufacturers proved the strength of this material in a convincing way.

358
00:29:35,981 --> 00:29:39,020
They fired a .357 Magnum at it.

359
00:29:39,620 --> 00:29:41,740
The bullet couldn't penetrate the plastic.

360
00:29:44,420 --> 00:29:50,180
But an even more important reason for using plastic was that it made power tools a lot safer.

361
00:29:53,480 --> 00:30:00,460
In the 1960s, new methods of machining plastic resins made it possible to double insulate tools.

362
00:30:00,461 --> 00:30:06,460
Now, besides the grounding wire, another layer of protection would be added to the motor.

363
00:30:06,461 --> 00:30:15,460
Right at the heart of our motors is the rotor, or what we call the armature, which is the moving part of the electric motor.

364
00:30:15,461 --> 00:30:24,460
A single insulated power tool would have pressed this lamination directly on the shaft, steel to steel.

365
00:30:24,461 --> 00:30:32,460
The problem with steel to steel contact is that if a short occurred inside the motor, it would shock the user.

366
00:30:32,461 --> 00:30:39,460
In a double insulated motor, the steel parts are separated by this white protective plastic resin.

367
00:30:39,461 --> 00:30:43,460
In its raw form, the resin looks like putty.

368
00:30:43,461 --> 00:30:47,460
It can even be rolled and molded like putty.

369
00:30:47,461 --> 00:30:50,960
Here, it's placed into a pressure heating machine.

370
00:30:50,961 --> 00:31:04,961
It will actually be baked onto motors, which in this case are for Craftsman Circular saw-ons.

371
00:31:05,080 --> 00:31:10,400
This all takes place at the Ryobi Power Tool Plant in Pickens, South Carolina.

372
00:31:10,401 --> 00:31:17,400
But similar procedures are used by most power tool manufacturers today.

373
00:31:17,401 --> 00:31:21,960
And even as these double insulated saws wind their way through the rest of the assembly

374
00:31:21,961 --> 00:31:31,360
process, they won't reach consumers until they're proven safe.

375
00:31:31,361 --> 00:31:38,140
Each tool is individually tested before going into its box.

376
00:31:38,141 --> 00:31:42,700
Samples of each different tool manufactured at Ryobi are pulled off the assembly line

377
00:31:42,701 --> 00:31:47,180
for even further testing.

378
00:31:47,181 --> 00:31:49,700
What happens to this sander is typical.

379
00:31:49,701 --> 00:31:53,800
This is called the six-foot drop test.

380
00:31:53,801 --> 00:32:02,900
Now the point of this test isn't to make sure the tool doesn't break when it's dropped.

381
00:32:02,901 --> 00:32:07,300
More importantly, it's to make sure that no damage has occurred in the motor that might

382
00:32:07,301 --> 00:32:10,380
lead to an electrical malfunction.

383
00:32:10,381 --> 00:32:18,060
That's the force, 1170 pounds, that that sander hit with.

384
00:32:19,600 --> 00:32:24,440
After the test, the tool is run on diagnostic equipment to assure its safety.

385
00:32:26,080 --> 00:32:29,640
Other problems could be detected by the sound the tool makes.

386
00:32:30,540 --> 00:32:34,920
Once the tester sets up a microphone, the sander is ready for a solo.

387
00:32:40,380 --> 00:32:48,680
Any unusual changes in the tone and pitch of the tool would indicate an operational

388
00:32:48,681 --> 00:32:51,580
problem that would have to be further investigated.

389
00:32:51,581 --> 00:32:57,180
You can't enter this room without heavy ear protection.

390
00:32:57,181 --> 00:33:06,680
A dozen or more power tools run continuously, sometimes for weeks at a time.

391
00:33:06,681 --> 00:33:11,760
The goal was to figure out how long each tool can run before having any motor problems.

392
00:33:15,780 --> 00:33:17,320
Ah, that's better.

393
00:33:18,400 --> 00:33:22,700
While plastic and such extensive testing made power tools better and safer,

394
00:33:23,320 --> 00:33:25,860
another great advance made them easier to use.

395
00:33:26,320 --> 00:33:29,160
It occurred when power tools shed their cords.

396
00:33:29,161 --> 00:33:39,760
beginning in world war ii battery technology was advancing some of the very first rechargeable

397
00:33:39,761 --> 00:33:46,060
batteries were used to trigger torpedoes from submarines and battleships i had been reading

398
00:33:46,061 --> 00:33:51,060
about these for quite some time and the improvement in the batteries was so substantial

399
00:33:51,061 --> 00:33:56,540
i kept wondering if we couldn't possibly make such an improvement in a power drill

400
00:33:56,541 --> 00:33:58,600
that could make use of batteries.

401
00:33:59,800 --> 00:34:04,220
In 1961, while many companies were exploring the possibility

402
00:34:04,221 --> 00:34:06,960
of introducing battery-operated power tools,

403
00:34:07,660 --> 00:34:11,180
Black & Decker is credited with bringing the first cordless tool,

404
00:34:11,560 --> 00:34:12,800
a drill, to market.

405
00:34:13,840 --> 00:34:17,740
It was powered by rechargeable nickel-cadmium batteries

406
00:34:17,741 --> 00:34:19,960
developed during World War II.

407
00:34:21,280 --> 00:34:24,880
The cordless drill sparked a revolution in the industry.

408
00:34:25,680 --> 00:34:31,040
It freed the user from being tied to a wall socket, but also had some disadvantages.

409
00:34:31,980 --> 00:34:38,880
The 4.8-volt batteries were not powerful enough for heavy-duty work and required overnight charging.

410
00:34:39,780 --> 00:34:44,120
It wouldn't be until the 1980s that these problems were solved.

411
00:34:44,121 --> 00:34:52,880
But first, in 1971, cordless technology, limited as it might be, would play a key role in space exploration.

412
00:34:52,881 --> 00:34:59,880
Astronauts Dave Scott and Jim Irwin took a cordless drill to the moon as part of their Apollo 15 mission.

413
00:34:59,881 --> 00:35:06,880
There, they would experience both the joys and the frustrations of early cordless tools.

414
00:35:09,880 --> 00:35:15,880
Using the drill to probe the lunar surface, they would conduct an extensive geological survey.

415
00:35:15,881 --> 00:35:24,880
While the drill's bits and battery pack were made by NASA, the power head was made by Black & Decker.

416
00:35:24,881 --> 00:35:30,880
The power head consists of the motor and the chuck, which is the device that grips the drill bit.

417
00:35:30,881 --> 00:35:38,880
Scott used the drill to acquire rock samples and to sink a heat-measuring probe deep into the lunar surface.

418
00:35:38,881 --> 00:35:44,660
But the drill, only about half as powerful as most of today's consumer drills,

419
00:35:45,320 --> 00:35:47,860
strained to bore through the soil and rock.

420
00:35:48,720 --> 00:35:53,600
In fact, Dave Scott would have bruised fingernails for several weeks after the mission

421
00:35:53,601 --> 00:35:56,320
because of the pressure he had to exert.

422
00:35:59,020 --> 00:36:02,180
Okay, Dave, take heart. You've got just one minute of drilling left.

423
00:36:02,760 --> 00:36:03,860
Okay, we may move.

424
00:36:03,861 --> 00:36:08,760
In spite of the strain, the drill succeeded in helping to retrieve samples

425
00:36:08,761 --> 00:36:11,360
from eight feet beneath the lunar surface.

426
00:36:12,360 --> 00:36:15,760
Five years later, America would celebrate its bicentennial.

427
00:36:16,360 --> 00:36:19,580
This year-long birthday party not only honored America,

428
00:36:20,080 --> 00:36:23,200
it sparked another great era of do-it-yourself.

429
00:36:23,980 --> 00:36:27,460
Among other things, the celebration would inspire Americans

430
00:36:27,461 --> 00:36:29,680
to refurbish historical sites.

431
00:36:29,681 --> 00:36:32,960
The effort would revitalize the do-it-yourself movement

432
00:36:32,961 --> 00:36:36,440
that was so prevalent in the years after World War II.

433
00:36:36,441 --> 00:36:39,940
It's time to talk to some of my favorite work, which is the sidewall.

434
00:36:39,941 --> 00:36:46,060
Perhaps nobody received more attention from their efforts than a Cuban-born architect named Bob Vila.

435
00:36:46,440 --> 00:36:51,840
The bicentennial in this country really got folks excited about polishing the brass doorknob,

436
00:36:52,460 --> 00:36:57,600
about cleaning the windows, about looking at their 100-year-old house and saying,

437
00:36:57,740 --> 00:37:01,920
this is a valuable piece of history. I've got to fix it up.

438
00:37:01,921 --> 00:37:09,560
Vila was involved in restoring a Victorian home that was so impressive it garnered national media coverage.

439
00:37:10,760 --> 00:37:22,120
Vila's uncanny ability to explain his art and technique in an interesting and informative way landed him his first television series, This Old House, in 1978.

440
00:37:22,121 --> 00:37:28,680
With the realities of the economy in the 70s and in the 80s, where a lot of people simply

441
00:37:28,681 --> 00:37:36,000
didn't have the money to hire craftsmen and artisans to come and do the paint job or do,

442
00:37:36,001 --> 00:37:39,400
you know, certain repair work for them that they had to do it themselves.

443
00:37:39,401 --> 00:37:45,740
And when we came along with the home improvement shows, we were kind of empowering them.

444
00:37:45,741 --> 00:37:50,400
Over the next 20 years, Vila would go on to host other do-it-yourself series, including

445
00:37:50,401 --> 00:37:55,900
home again and become the standard for at least a half dozen similar series to follow.

446
00:37:56,900 --> 00:38:03,100
By the early 1980s, Vila's wide audience of do-it-yourselfers were in the market for newer,

447
00:38:03,560 --> 00:38:04,846
better power tools.

448
00:38:04,847 --> 00:38:09,920
In response, several power tool manufacturers introduced new batteries that

449
00:38:09,921 --> 00:38:16,140
were not only more powerful, but were interchangeable between tools like drills, sanders, and jigsaws.

450
00:38:16,141 --> 00:38:23,140
Now the industry was producing easier to use, safer, and more versatile cordless tools than ever before.

451
00:38:23,141 --> 00:38:30,140
Yet the thirst for more power was about to push power tools to yet another plateau.

452
00:38:33,140 --> 00:38:41,140
The battery was invented in 1800 by Italian physicist Alessandro Volta, for whom the Volt is named.

453
00:38:46,140 --> 00:38:51,760
Well, let's face it.

454
00:38:51,780 --> 00:38:54,840
We can come up with all sorts of good reasons for using power tools.

455
00:38:55,240 --> 00:38:57,880
We save money by doing home improvement jobs ourselves.

456
00:38:58,040 --> 00:39:00,280
We can work more quickly and more efficiently.

457
00:39:00,940 --> 00:39:03,680
We can build little birdhouses for our feathered friends.

458
00:39:04,080 --> 00:39:04,780
But you know what?

459
00:39:04,781 --> 00:39:08,960
The truth is most people just think more power is really cool.

460
00:39:16,140 --> 00:39:26,860
On almost any given weekend, cities across the country play host to do-it-yourself fairs, like this one outside Cleveland, Ohio.

461
00:39:27,840 --> 00:39:31,400
Here, do-it-yourself gurus come to inspire the crowds.

462
00:39:32,260 --> 00:39:38,120
It's also a chance for toolmakers to show off the powerful capabilities of their newest tools.

463
00:39:38,840 --> 00:39:40,260
Fantastic machine.

464
00:39:40,760 --> 00:39:46,120
Remember Albert Kaufman who converted his wife's sewing machine to a jigsaw for the Bosch Company?

465
00:39:46,740 --> 00:39:51,760
Well, today, the company is known as Skill Bosch, and it's still making jigsaws.

466
00:39:53,560 --> 00:39:59,700
But now jigsaws can take a power plunge like this and still make an intricate cut.

467
00:40:00,800 --> 00:40:05,000
Remember Art Emmons who invented the takeabout sander in 1926?

468
00:40:05,001 --> 00:40:10,640
Well, it's no longer called the takeabout, but Porter Cable's newest belt sander still

469
00:40:10,641 --> 00:40:16,620
has Art Emmons creative fingerprints all over it, including the basics of the design and

470
00:40:16,621 --> 00:40:18,620
the dust collector.

471
00:40:18,621 --> 00:40:25,120
Remember A.H. Peterson, who invented the hole shooter for Henry Ford?

472
00:40:25,121 --> 00:40:31,000
Well now the company that emerged, Milwaukee Tools, is best known for the Sawzall.

473
00:40:31,001 --> 00:40:36,080
You take the saws off, you push it against the material you're cutting with the light

474
00:40:36,081 --> 00:40:37,080
pressure on the top.

475
00:40:37,081 --> 00:40:41,760
You should never have to overload the tool at all, but the tool did the work.

476
00:40:41,761 --> 00:40:46,520
Contractors have loved this tool since it was first introduced in the 1950s because

477
00:40:46,521 --> 00:40:53,500
it cuts through darn near anything at all sorts of angles.

478
00:40:53,501 --> 00:40:58,700
Meanwhile the innovator of the pistol grip drill, Black & Decker, offers a cordless power

479
00:40:58,701 --> 00:41:02,008
tool line with batteries that exceed 14 volts.

480
00:41:02,009 --> 00:41:05,100
Throughout most of the 90s, this much power

481
00:41:05,101 --> 00:41:10,280
was reserved for the professionals. Now, it's marketed to consumers.

482
00:41:10,281 --> 00:41:12,260
Instead of the traditional

483
00:41:12,261 --> 00:41:17,460
nickel cadmium batteries, these tools are powered by nickel metal hydride, a chemical

484
00:41:17,461 --> 00:41:21,580
compound used for cell phones and laptop computers.

485
00:41:21,581 --> 00:41:28,180
For years, that chemical compound did not fit well with power tools because although

486
00:41:28,181 --> 00:41:29,980
It yielded great run time.

487
00:41:29,981 --> 00:41:32,480
It didn't do a great job from a power standpoint.

488
00:41:32,481 --> 00:41:34,600
But what we did is we worked with our battery supplier,

489
00:41:34,601 --> 00:41:36,120
in this case Panasonic,

490
00:41:36,121 --> 00:41:39,540
and have developed kind of a breakthrough for power tools.

491
00:41:39,541 --> 00:41:40,860
The development of batteries

492
00:41:40,861 --> 00:41:43,180
that offer the longevity of a cell phone

493
00:41:43,181 --> 00:41:47,200
with the power to spin this 24 volt circular saw

494
00:41:47,201 --> 00:41:49,940
seems an appropriate way to end 100 years

495
00:41:49,941 --> 00:41:51,940
of power tool history.

496
00:41:51,941 --> 00:41:53,300
But not quite.

497
00:41:53,301 --> 00:41:56,860
Because a new name, one associated with not only tools,

498
00:41:56,861 --> 00:42:00,920
But laughter has joined the fraternity of power tool manufacturers.

499
00:42:02,120 --> 00:42:04,420
It's a cutthroat business, this tool business.

500
00:42:04,640 --> 00:42:07,120
I think they all get along, but I guess it's just like the other,

501
00:42:07,220 --> 00:42:09,780
Stanley and Black & Decker and all the companies.

502
00:42:10,120 --> 00:42:12,380
They're very competitive, and now all of a sudden we're a player.

503
00:42:12,940 --> 00:42:16,340
Tim Allen is still an actor, as evidenced by the action hero makeup

504
00:42:16,341 --> 00:42:18,200
he's wearing on this film set.

505
00:42:18,700 --> 00:42:21,940
However, he isn't acting anymore when it comes to tools.

506
00:42:22,680 --> 00:42:26,320
In 1999, Allen launched his own line of power tools.

507
00:42:26,321 --> 00:42:32,700
They include a jigsaw, sander, and cordless drill.

508
00:42:33,200 --> 00:42:35,400
His inspiration for launching the line?

509
00:42:36,060 --> 00:42:36,900
Salad dressing.

510
00:42:37,520 --> 00:42:41,180
Alan is using his passion for tools to raise money for charity,

511
00:42:41,540 --> 00:42:45,060
just as actor Paul Newman has done with his food products.

512
00:42:45,620 --> 00:42:49,600
You look up to this guy because he's done so much in his career that I would certainly aspire to.

513
00:42:50,280 --> 00:42:53,020
And I was curious one day about why he got into that.

514
00:42:53,021 --> 00:43:00,520
I had the same sensibilities, that it's a great way to employ even more people, get people active in charity work.

515
00:43:01,160 --> 00:43:06,520
Alan learned about motors and bearings by refurbishing abandoned washing machines as a teenager.

516
00:43:07,160 --> 00:43:12,460
He also used his own background in design to give the tools a personal touch.

517
00:43:12,840 --> 00:43:14,840
I didn't want to just sign my name on something.

518
00:43:15,180 --> 00:43:18,200
I wanted to have some input in how the thing was designed, because I love design.

519
00:43:18,300 --> 00:43:20,120
I love the whole art of design.

520
00:43:20,380 --> 00:43:21,540
I love the science of design.

521
00:43:21,541 --> 00:43:23,700
and some of my favorite heroes are designers,

522
00:43:24,140 --> 00:43:25,940
so I wanted to be able to get involved in that process.

523
00:43:26,560 --> 00:43:29,320
Allen worked with a design team from the Ryobi Corporation,

524
00:43:29,560 --> 00:43:31,780
a toolmaker that manufactures the line.

525
00:43:33,060 --> 00:43:37,740
His power tools reflect many of the changes the industry has made in the last decade

526
00:43:37,741 --> 00:43:41,680
to make power tools more user-friendly than ever before.

527
00:43:43,320 --> 00:43:45,640
A slot holds extra drill bits.

528
00:43:45,641 --> 00:43:48,240
The grip is ergonomically designed,

529
00:43:48,241 --> 00:43:52,780
meaning it's made to feel comfortable and ballast in the user's hand.

530
00:43:53,500 --> 00:43:57,320
And a bubble level helps identify a perfect 90-degree angle.

531
00:43:58,540 --> 00:44:02,400
Such concerns for convenience would probably seem a bit arcane

532
00:44:02,401 --> 00:44:06,640
to the men who invented the first electric drill 100 years earlier,

533
00:44:07,120 --> 00:44:09,680
or to Alonzo Decker and Duncan Black,

534
00:44:09,681 --> 00:44:13,080
who designed the pistol grip drill a short time later.

535
00:44:14,060 --> 00:44:17,700
But they also carry on the spirit of these early innovators.

536
00:44:18,240 --> 00:44:23,960
whose goals included making our lives a bit easier and much more productive.

537
00:44:28,600 --> 00:44:32,380
You know, I'm really struck by how good-looking power tools are today.

538
00:44:32,800 --> 00:44:36,860
I could use this to drill a hole, or I could put it on my fireplace as a work of art.

539
00:44:37,660 --> 00:44:40,780
But to the old-timers, a power tool was for nothing but work.

540
00:44:41,040 --> 00:44:44,660
About 50 years ago, a friend of Porter Cable Engineer Art Emmons

541
00:44:44,661 --> 00:44:47,960
took his dull metal take-about sander, like this one.

542
00:44:48,240 --> 00:44:52,080
had it polished to a beautiful shine, and presented it to art as a gift.

543
00:44:52,680 --> 00:44:55,360
Emmons took one look at it, shook his head, and said,

544
00:44:55,860 --> 00:44:56,960
You ruined it!

545
00:44:58,180 --> 00:45:00,760
For Modern Marvels, I'm Ron Hazleton.

